WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.509 --> 00:00:06.120
At first glance, The Hogeweyk looks a lot
like any other neighborhood in the Netherlands.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.331
It’s got a restaurant, barber shop, theater,
grocery store, and open green space.

00:00:11.331 --> 00:00:14.892
You can see people walking around, getting
groceries, having coffee.

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:20.988
The difference is that this neighborhood is
a facility for people with severe dementia.

00:00:20.988 --> 00:00:22.140
And everyone else in it

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.560
from grocery store cashiers to barbers 
to waiters

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.758
are trained in dementia care.

00:00:27.758 --> 00:00:31.497
This model of care has been dubbed 
a "dementia village".

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:34.059
As people continue to live longer 
around the world,

00:00:34.059 --> 00:00:38.149
the proportion of older people in the population
will continue to rise

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:42.222
and that means a steep increase in 
the number of people with dementia.

00:00:42.222 --> 00:00:45.350
Caring for that population will require 
designing environments

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.549
that help people feel safe and free

00:00:47.549 --> 00:00:50.716
even as they lose the ability to recognize
the world around them.

00:00:50.716 --> 00:00:52.779
And the architects behind dementia villages

00:00:52.779 --> 00:00:55.287
think they might just have figured out
the formula to do it.

00:00:59.482 --> 00:01:00.909
This is The Hogeweyk.

00:01:00.909 --> 00:01:04.590
Social life is happening there 
so you can see it now.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.235
That's Eloy van Hal, one of 
The Hogeweyk's founders.

00:01:07.235 --> 00:01:10.000
Dementia, it's — of course — a brain disease.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.670
If you look at your life as a photo album

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.350
you forget the last pictures.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.520
The last pictures, they fall out of the album
and so you remember the past better

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:20.952
when you were younger.

00:01:20.952 --> 00:01:26.528
And that's a daily challenge for the individuals:
how to live with memory loss.

00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:31.025
In early stages, people with dementia can
live at home, looked after by family, friends

00:01:31.025 --> 00:01:32.685
or home care

00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:35.119
but they eventually need full-time care.

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:37.784
Often, that care comes from traditional 
nursing homes.

00:01:37.784 --> 00:01:40.427
But those settings can be sterile and clinical.

00:01:40.427 --> 00:01:43.219
You all dine together in a big dining area.

00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:46.522
You all have to listen to the same music 
at the same time.

00:01:46.522 --> 00:01:51.364
Where you're forced as a person into a program 
of the institution.

00:01:51.364 --> 00:01:56.179
There's not that much attention to 
who are you really

00:01:56.179 --> 00:01:59.960
what is my life's story, who am I, 
what do I prefer

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.978
to do during the whole day.

00:02:01.978 --> 00:02:04.139
In any setting, the goal 
of good dementia care

00:02:04.139 --> 00:02:07.635
should be to preserve quality of life
as dementia progresses.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.141
In traditional settings, like nursing homes

00:02:10.141 --> 00:02:12.702
surface-level changes are 
a good place to start.

00:02:12.702 --> 00:02:15.879
Since people with dementia might perceive
dark tiles as holes

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:19.422
floors should be visually consistent,
without contrasting patterns.

00:02:19.422 --> 00:02:22.766
Brightly colored doors and handrails help
residents navigate around

00:02:22.766 --> 00:02:26.793
and bright dishware has been shown to help
people with Alzheimer’s eat more food.

00:02:26.793 --> 00:02:30.459
Acoustic ceiling tiles, carpeted floors,
and soft furniture

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:33.319
can absorb noises that trigger disorientation.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:36.159
Glass-fronted cabinets help residents have
a clear line of sight

00:02:36.159 --> 00:02:37.840
to what’s stored inside.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.590
And lighting fixtures that mimic natural light

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:43.159
strengthen residents’ circadian rhythms,
which helps mitigate sundowning

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:47.036
where people with dementia become confused
in the evening and night.

00:02:47.036 --> 00:02:52.629
Some of the advice about lighting, clear lines,
surface patterns, and so

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:56.380
are based on institutions.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:03.374
Instead of normalizing the environments, all
those elements are essential.

00:03:03.374 --> 00:03:07.357
But sometimes they are the solution 
for an institution.

00:03:07.357 --> 00:03:10.010
But The Hogeweyk’s design solution is different.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:14.159
Instead of changing surface-level design to
preserve quality of life for people with dementia

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:16.540
they took a structural approach

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.989
and designed their facility to look as much
like the outside world as possible.

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:23.480
Our guiding principle here is normalcy.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.777
It’s the vision of a normal life for people
living with dementia.

00:03:26.777 --> 00:03:29.517
That starts with lodging.

00:03:29.517 --> 00:03:33.113
Traditional nursing homes keep all their residents
under one roof.

00:03:33.113 --> 00:03:36.599
But real households don’t typically consist
of dozens of people.

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:40.904
So, The Hogeweyk split its residents up 
into small groups of 6 or 7

00:03:40.904 --> 00:03:43.473
recreating the scale of a single family home.

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:47.768
Inside, each resident has a private room
furnished with personal belongings.

00:03:47.768 --> 00:03:53.459
The units are split up into different neighborhoods
to mimic Dutch cities residents are used to.

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:56.670
The public space, the gardens, the streets,
the squares.

00:03:57.461 --> 00:04:02.280
They are equally important if you want to
build a community where people live.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.430
Each neighborhood’s public space was given
distinct landscapes and unique landmarks

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.924
so people could easily find their way around.

00:04:08.924 --> 00:04:12.490
And destinations like the theater, barber
shop, and grocery store

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:14.216
were put in separate buildings

00:04:14.216 --> 00:04:19.100
encouraging intentional movement and intermingling
in that public space.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.410
These different areas provide multiple scales
of experience

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.330
and residents are given the autonomy to safely
wander along that spectrum

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.463
from very private to very public space.

00:04:29.706 --> 00:04:32.500
That autonomy helps further preserve 
quality of life.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.585
People stay in their own bedroom.

00:04:34.585 --> 00:04:37.662
Many people socialize in the living room.

00:04:37.662 --> 00:04:39.950
But you can also decide to leave the house

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:44.450
because the front door is open and to walk
to your own private outdoor space

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.380
your terrace or balcony or your own garden

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.290
or you walk further into the neighborhood
where you can mingle.

00:04:51.548 --> 00:04:54.970
So, it's about choice, choice, choice:

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:58.390
where you want to be during the whole day
and with whom.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:00.680
Crucially, The Hogeweyk’s design allows
for

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:03.920
a balance of safe design and controlled risk.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.610
Walkways, for example, don't have 
super high walls

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:08.570
to guarantee no one falls over.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.240
You see handrails everywhere in institutions.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.807
The question is, do you need a handrail everywhere?

00:05:13.807 --> 00:05:19.048
Or do you want to provide a walker, where
you can walk with two hands on the walker,

00:05:19.048 --> 00:05:21.241
and make it accessible for the walker?

00:05:21.241 --> 00:05:23.502
Since The Hogeweyk opened in 2009

00:05:23.502 --> 00:05:26.640
dozens of other dementia villages have 
opened across the globe.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.180
But without ample government funding,
they can be prohibitively expensive.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.590
And there isn’t enough evidence yet to say
whether the dementia village model is better

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.660
than traditional models of care.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.760
The Hogeweyk claims that since they transitioned
from a traditional model to the village

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.290
the number of residents on antipsychotic medication
decreased from 50 percent in 1993

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:47.376
to just 8-10 percent today.

00:05:47.376 --> 00:05:51.448
And there have been studies backing up different
features that dementia villages use.

00:05:51.448 --> 00:05:54.210
One review of the evidence found that 
uncrowded, small-scale living

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:59.220
resulted in fewer psychiatric symptoms and
behavioral issues among residents with dementia.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.350
And that increased daylight lighting reduced
their behavioral issues

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:04.500
and improved spatial orientation.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.350
Another review found that outdoor gardens
can reduce agitation

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.950
and improve quality of life 
for people with dementia.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:15.360
But by creating an environment specifically
for people with cognitive and physical impairments

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.409
dementia villages are a fascinating practice
in universal design:

00:06:19.409 --> 00:06:23.891
design that works for as many people as possible,
regardless of their ability.

00:06:23.891 --> 00:06:27.899
And they can teach us something about where
the outside world falls short.

00:06:27.899 --> 00:06:32.750
If you design well for normal people 
without dementia

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.334
you design also well for people with dementia.

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:39.640
We forgot that people with dementia 
are human beings

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.292
with a lot of aspirations.

00:06:41.292 --> 00:06:44.330
We forgot that people with dementia 
are not patients

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:47.620
where you can put them in a chair 
and wait until they die.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.812
They are people who want to do certain things
during the day.

00:06:50.812 --> 00:06:53.748
And they know and they're happy 
to have this freedom.

